## FAUST-STK physical models — STK-4.3 (MIT-style), permitted

`engine/faust/dsp/stk_guitar.dsp` and `engine/faust/dsp/stk_piano.dsp` are
derived from the **FAUST Synthesis ToolKit** (`examples/physicalModeling/
faust-stk/` in https://github.com/grame-cncm/faust), by **Romain Michon**
(CIEREC EA 3068, Université Jean Monnet / CCRMA, Stanford) with **Julius O.
Smith III** — a Faust port of instruments from the Synthesis ToolKit and the
SynthBuilder models. Every source file in that directory carries
`declare licence "STK-4.3"`, the license of **The Synthesis ToolKit in C++**,
© 1995–2023 **Perry R. Cook and Gary P. Scavone**
(https://ccrma.stanford.edu/software/stk/ — full text at
https://github.com/thestk/stk/blob/master/LICENSE).

STK-4.3 is an MIT-style permission grant: use, copy, modify, merge, publish,
distribute, sublicense and sell, provided the copyright notice and permission
notice travel with the work — which is what this section is. It adds one
NON-BINDING request, that modifications be sent back to the original developer
so they can be folded into the canonical version; the license says in its own
words that this is "not a binding provision". It is compatible with this
repository's MIT grant, so no carve-out is needed: unlike lamejs and eSpeak NG
above, nothing here is excluded.

`engine/faust/dsp/erhu.dsp` (2026-08-30) takes ONE function from the same
lineage through the standard library rather than by copying: `pm.bowTable` in
`physmodels.lib`, the STK/Smith friction curve `pow(|v|+0.75, -4)`. Everything
else in that file — the two terminations, the string selection, the membrane
body and its Bessel-zero mode ratios, the output stage — is this project's own,
and its header says which sentence of the source article produced each line.

What was taken and what was changed is written at the top of each `.dsp` file.
In summary: `stk_guitar.dsp` uses the extended Karplus-Strong string of
`NLFeks.dsp` (Smith's "Virtual Electric Guitars in Faust",
https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/vegf.html) with its dynamic-level
filter reconnected, feeding this project's own pickup, preamp and cabinet;
`stk_piano.dsp` is the commuted waveguide piano of `piano.dsp`/`piano1.dsp`
(David Braun's pure-Faust revision, which replaces the C++ `ffunction` lookup
tables with Faust `waveform` tables and is what makes it compilable to
WebAssembly at all) with its per-key hammer velocity crossfade reconnected, a
fitted tuning correction, and a mono, reverb-free output stage. The synthesis
— every coefficient table, every filter topology — is the toolkit's. Thanks to
Romain Michon, Julius Smith, David Braun, Perry Cook and Gary Scavone.

